WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.660
- Lemme know if you've ever
been in this situation.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.220
You're sitting down writing something,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.610
and you've been struggling for hours

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.700
trying to find the right words.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.220
It's super painful and frustrating,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.670
and no good ideas are coming to your head.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.860
But all of a sudden, something hits you.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.510
The right words magically
seem to just come to you.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:24.200
It's like you've been mining
in a cold, dark cave for days,

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.100
and you finally struck gold.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.000
Or maybe you're playing
around on the piano

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.150
trying to improvise something,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.460
and you're not really coming
up with anything good.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.570
But then all of a sudden
you try something new,

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.650
and you somehow pull off
a brilliant new melody.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.510
It's like genius struck
for a brief moment,

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.360
and then faded from existence.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.930
Moments like these seem to
fall upon us kind of randomly

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.090
and without warning.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.800
While we're in them, it's almost euphoric.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.960
Things kind of click together
and start making sense,

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.040
like the pieces to a giant complex puzzle

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.510
are starting to snap themselves together.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.410
But most of the time, this
just isn't how things go.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.100
Practicing an instrument is
usually tedious and boring.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.750
Writing is difficult.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.350
But as we're trudging
through these activities,

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.320
we remember those brief
bouts of brilliance

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.130
and we hope that this time,

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.170
when we're participating in our craft,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.240
it can be like that one time
when things just clicked.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.750
Although they may seem like it,

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.770
these little flashes of brilliance

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.410
aren't just random and nonsensical.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.440
They are a window, through
which we can catch a glimpse

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.380
of our potential.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.730
It's like a little preview of the power

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.040
which we all possess inside of us.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.280
A power that makes this elusive state

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.140
of brilliance and creativity

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.180
into a consistent and replicable reality.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.680
And if we're able to
fully develop this power,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.570
it can give us a greater
command over ourselves,

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.250
over others, and over the world around us.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.653
This power is called mastery.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.420
Now when most people
think of the word mastery,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.830
they think of somebody who's
really good at something.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.170
Beethoven was a master
composer and musician.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.670
And most people would
say that Martin Scorsese

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.100
is a master filmmaker.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.530
But mastery is a lot more

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.630
than just being really good at something.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.650
Because the master has such
a thorough understanding

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.150
of his craft, that it becomes
like an extension of himself.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.260
It becomes a language they can use

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.710
to influence and inspire other people

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.150
in a way that seems almost magical.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.960
A masterpiece of literature
can bring us to tears of joy

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.220
or tears of bitter sadness.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.830
And a master painting
can cause us to reflect

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.720
on the fleeting nature of time,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.950
or the nature of our very existence.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.370
It's as if the master,
through their expertise,

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.990
has access to something
like the heart of life.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.570
But a lot of people disqualify themselves

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.140
from ever achieving mastery
in whatever their craft is.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.600
You know, if they're a
normal person like you or me,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.710
we tend to thing that we're
incapable of achieving mastery

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.600
like some of the greats before us.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.810
We may be tempted to think that mastery

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.260
is reserved for the genius
or the child prodigy.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.510
But the truth is mastery is something

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.640
that already exists inside you,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.580
and it's up to you to develop it.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:14.310
So how does one go about
developing their mastery?

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.790
Well you may have heard
that it takes 10,000 hours

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.030
of practice to become
a master at something.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.780
And this is kind of true.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.840
You do need an insane amount of practice

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.810
at your given craft in
order to achieve mastery.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.640
But it isn't just a numbers game.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.480
You're probably never going
to put in that amount of time

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.350
practicing a craft if you
don't actually enjoy it.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.610
Which is why the first step of mastery

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.270
is to discover your calling.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.860
Everyone who has ever lived
has at least one thing

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.350
that sets their imagination on fire.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.350
Something we feel naturally
drawn towards or fascinated by.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.710
Whether that's constructing something,

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.690
or solving a problem, or
playing an instrument,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.220
because of your unique brain,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.250
you have certain things
you really dislike doing

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.870
and certain things that you
really have a preference for.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.310
So really reflect on your life

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:06.000
and figure out where your passions lie.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.790
If you really like doing something

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.680
and you feel an importance while doing it,

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.300
then that's an indication
that you can potentially

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.220
become a master in this thing.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.140
But passion alone won't get you far.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.350
The second step on the road to mastery

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.243
is to eliminate the ego.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.180
Realize that you know absolutely nothing,

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.910
and become a blank canvas.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.950
You need to seek every opportunity to grow

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:34.450
and view everything as a
potential learning experience.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:37.490
Approach your craft with
a ruthless objectivity.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.130
Don't identify as being good
or talented at anything.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.010
Because that puts a barrier in the way

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.160
of viewing your work objectively,

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.600
and being critical about it.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.880
A good example of me not doing this

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.370
was when I was in a filmmaking class.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.440
And I always carried
around this sense of pride

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.140
about my filmmaking ability.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.820
I was usually the best
filmmaker in a room.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.280
But I'm usually in a room
where nobody makes films,

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.300
so that's pretty easy.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:05.010
My now good friend Jared also made films.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.450
Hey Jared, you're gonna
be in my video, okay?

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.610
- Okay, right now?

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:10.950
- Before I knew him really well,

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.350
he would present his work to the class,

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:14.770
and it was pretty good.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:16.690
And I would feel threatened by this,

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:19.150
because I'm supposed
to be a good filmmaker.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.930
And for some reason, his work being good

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.820
was a threat to that?

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.180
It doesn't make any sense.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.550
But that's because the ego is involved.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.490
You need to eliminate the need

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.910
to be the best artist in the room,

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.640
or the best at your craft,
or the best athlete.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.280
Because when you're trying
to learn as much as possible,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.660
none of that matters.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.330
If you can eliminate the ego,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.990
then you can view the work of your peers,

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.880
and figure out what they're
doing better than you are.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.880
And then you can apply
that to your own work.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.300
It also allows you to
look at your own work

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.940
with a ruthless objectivity as well,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.703
and scrutinize it more.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.080
This video's pretty trash.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.770
Find out where your weak spots are,

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.860
and try to make them strengths.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.750
None of this is possible
if you have a giant ego,

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.570
and identify as a master long

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.170
before you've earned that title.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.800
Step Number Three on the road to mastery

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.540
is to learn to embrace tedium.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.060
When you first approach a craft,

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.460
you approach it as an apprentice.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.960
Practicing your craft, for the most part,

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.000
is going to be tedious.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.990
It's not going to be very exciting.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.900
Applying new techniques and trying

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.000
to stretch your boundaries is
usually really uncomfortable,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.133
boring, and not very exciting.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:36.750
Why does that look so bad?

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.650
But if you learn to
embrace the tedious nature

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.530
of practicing your craft day after day,

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.720
you'll eventually reach a
point of accelerated return.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.050
You start to become a practitioner.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.720
And the practitioner understands
the complex relationships

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.900
between each component of their craft.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.120
They start to get a view
of the bigger picture.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.830
They can really engage with their craft

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.370
in a more creative way.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.450
Once you reach this stage in the game,

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:06.100
practicing becomes a lot less
tedious, and more enjoyable.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.910
You can start to have some fun with it.

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.460
But the road doesn't end there.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.580
You still need to propel
yourself into mastery.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.090
You still need to find
that deeper understanding.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.786
And one of the most effective
ways you can do this

00:07:18.786 --> 00:07:20.610
is move on to Step Number Four,

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.060
which is absorb the master's power.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:24.860
Since you've become a practitioner,

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.780
you can use your competency in your craft

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.880
as sort of a proof that
you take it seriously,

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.220
and you're willing to learn.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.550
You can then take this proof to somebody

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.150
who's much better than you at your craft,

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:38.300
and work under them.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.310
It's crucial that you find somebody

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.640
who's much better than you at what you do,

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:43.770
and learn from them,

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:46.170
so that you don't have
to reinvent the wheel.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.110
Your work can also be
scrutinized by somebody

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.410
who has the authority to scrutinize it.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:53.160
By learning from a master,

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.110
you can then view your own
work through their eyes,

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:57.600
and try to work on things

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.250
that they would want you to work on.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.680
And working alongside a
master collaboratively

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.050
is a great way to become one yourself.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:08.360
But if you're unwilling to
take this path towards mastery,

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.470
then this creative power inside
you will remain undeveloped.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:13.960
It will lie dormant.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.730
It'll occasionally reveal itself

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.000
in these little glimmers
of temporary brilliance,

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.686
begging to be fully realized.

00:08:21.686 --> 00:08:24.436
